## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical cost optimization, data retention, and basic governance using Azure Storage lifecycle rules.

## Key Concepts

- Lifecycle Management rules
- Blob tiers and retention
- Soft delete and versioning
- Cost Management budgets and alerts

## Code Example

```json
{
  "rules": [
    {
      "name": "MoveToCool",
      "enabled": true,
      "type": "Lifecycle",
      "definition": {
        "filters": {
          "blobTypes": ["blockBlob"],
          "prefixMatch": ["images/"]
        },
        "actions": {
          "baseBlob": {
            "tierToCool": {"daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 30},
            "delete": {"daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 90}
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the lifecycle policy in a non-destructive way?
- What are trade-offs of Cool vs Archive tiers for image data?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical understanding of observability in a lightweight Azure stack, covering frontend-backend instrumentation, tracing continuity, log routing, and alerting, plus a minimal CI/CD workflow.

## Key Concepts

- Application Insights integration in SPA and Azure Functions
- End-to-end tracing with correlation IDs
- Log Analytics workspace and log routing
- Alerts for latency spikes and error rates
- Cost considerations for observability including sampling strategies
- CI/CD wiring with GitHub Actions for Azure Static Web Apps and Functions

## Code Example

```javascript
// SPA: generate correlation ID and pass in requests
const correlationId = crypto.randomUUID();

// Store in session for consistency
sessionStorage.setItem('correlationId', correlationId);

// Include in API requests
fetch('/api/data', {
  headers: {
    'x-correlation-id': correlationId
  }
});
```

```javascript
// Azure Function: read and log correlation ID
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const correlationId = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || crypto.randomUUID();

  context.log.info('Processing request', { correlationId });

  context.res = {
    body: { data: [] },
    headers: { 'x-correlation-id': correlationId }
  };
};
```

## Cost Considerations

- **Application Insights**: Starts with 5 GB free per month; implement sampling (typically 10-30%) for high-traffic apps
- **Log Analytics**: Pay-per-GB ingestion; retention defaults to 30 days
- **Azure Static Web Apps**: Free tier available
- **Azure Functions**: Consumption plan with pay-per-execution pricing
- **Alerts**: First 10,000 alerts are free monthly

## Why This Is Asked
This angle tests practical integration of real-time UX with common Azure PaaS: static hosting, serverless APIs, scalable data, and live updates, plus governance and disaster recovery.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Static Web Apps with SPA routing
- Azure Functions (Consumption) as API layer
- Cosmos DB SQL API as scalable data store
- Azure SignalR Service for real-time client updates
- Azure Entra ID for authentication
- Azure Front Door with TLS termination
- GitHub Actions for CI/CD
- Resource tagging and cost budgeting
- Cross-region DR for Cosmos DB

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Azure Function (JavaScript) emitting a SignalR update
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const update = { orderId: req.params.id, status: req.body.status };
  context.bindings.signalRMessages = [{
    target: 'orderUpdated',
    arguments: [ update ]
  }];
  context.res = { status: 200 };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test latency of real-time updates across regions?
- What security practices would you apply for API keys and secrets in this stack?
